The Tragedy of Fibonacci, Prince of Recurrence.

Romeo, a young man who is forever the number that came just before.
Juliet, a lady who is always exactly one step ahead of her beloved.
Ophelia, a maiden charged with counting down to something dreadful.
The Ghost, a spectre with an unwholesome appetite for arithmetic.


                    Act I: A Recurrence In Four Scenes.

                    Scene I: Two Lovers Take Their Marks.

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Romeo:
 You are as fine as a rose.

Juliet:
 You are as rotten as nothing.

[Exeunt]


                    Scene II: The Ghost Demands A Number.

[Enter The Ghost and Ophelia]

The Ghost:
 You are as mighty as a fine fair sweet golden mighty noble rose. You are as
 noble as the sum of yourself and a brave bold fine hero. You are as brave as
 the sum of yourself and a brave fine hero. You are as proud as the sum of
 yourself and a fine hero. Speak your mind!

The Ghost:
 You are as sweet as a fine fair sweet golden mighty noble rose. You are as
 sorry as the difference between yourself and a rose. Speak your mind!

The Ghost:
 You are as pretty as a fine fair sweet golden mighty rose. Speak your mind!

The Ghost:
 Listen to your heart. Are you worse than a rose? If so, let us proceed to
 Scene IV.


                    Scene III: The Long Climb, One Rung At A Time.

[Exeunt]

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Romeo:
 You are as good as the sum of yourself and me.

Juliet:
 You are as big as the difference between me and yourself.

[Exeunt]

[Enter The Ghost and Ophelia]

The Ghost:
 You are as bad as the difference between yourself and a rose. Are you as good
 as nothing? If not, let us return to Scene III.


                    Scene IV: Romeo Is Asked To Speak Plainly For Once.

[Exeunt]

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Juliet:
 Open your heart! You are as small as the sum of a brave bold fine hero and a
 fine hero. Speak your mind!

[Exeunt]
